      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is an agent skill?</div><div class="a">A directory containing a SKILL.md file, which tells an AI agent how to do a specific job. Optional folders alongside it hold scripts, reference documents and assets. The format was created by Anthropic in October 2025 and published as an open standard that December, and several agents from different companies now read it.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is SKILL.md?</div><div class="a">A Markdown file with a YAML header. The header carries the metadata and the body carries the instructions, with no format restrictions on the body. The portable specification defines six header fields: name, description, license, compatibility, metadata and allowed-tools. Only name and description are required.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is the difference between agent skills and MCP?</div><div class="a">MCP connects an agent to data and systems; a skill teaches it what to do with them. The vendor summary is that MCP connects Claude to data while skills teach Claude what to do with that data. They are complementary rather than competing, and a skill can be written to drive an MCP connection.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is the difference between skills and agents?</div><div class="a">A skill loads into the conversation you are already in. A subagent is a separate assistant that works in its own context and returns a summary. Use a skill when you want the work visible and steerable, and a subagent when you want a self-contained task handled and reported back.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is the difference between skills and plugins?</div><div class="a">A plugin is the packaging, a skill is the content. A plugin is a distributable bundle that can contain skills, subagents, hooks and MCP configuration together. Vendor guidance suggests standalone skills for personal workflows and plugins for sharing with teammates, versioned releases and community distribution.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Do agent skills use up the context window?</div><div class="a">Barely, until used. Only the name and description load at session start, at roughly 100 tokens per skill. The instructions load on activation and are recommended to stay under 5,000 tokens. Bundled scripts run in the shell so only their output enters the conversation.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How do you install a Claude Code skill?</div><div class="a">Place the folder in a skills directory: <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> for all projects, or <code>.claude/skills/</code> for one project. Easier in practice is to unzip it anywhere and ask the agent to install the skills from that folder. Marketplaces install through a plugin command instead.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Where do agent skills live on disk?</div><div class="a">It depends on the tool, because the specification standardises the file and not the location. A convention has formed around <code>.agents/skills/</code> for cross-tool sharing, and many agents also read <code>.claude/skills/</code>. When a skill is not detected, the wrong directory is the usual explanation.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Which AI tools support agent skills?</div><div class="a">Support is confirmed in each vendor's own documentation for Claude Code, the Claude apps and API, the Claude Agent SDK, Cowork, ChatGPT and Codex, GitHub Copilot, VS Code, Cursor, goose and Kimi CLI, among others. Writing to the six portable fields is what keeps a skill working across them.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Are agent skills an open standard?</div><div class="a">Yes in license and contribution, with a caveat. The code is Apache 2.0 and the documentation CC BY 4.0, and the repository describes the format as originally developed by Anthropic and released as an open standard. Unlike MCP it has no neutral governance body and publishes no version number.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Are agent skills safe to install?</div><div class="a">Only from sources you trust. A study of 31,132 published skills found 26.1% contained at least one vulnerability, and skills bundling executable scripts were 2.12 times more likely to be affected. The vendor warning is that a malicious skill can direct an agent to run code that does not match its stated purpose.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How do you check a skill before running it?</div><div class="a">Read it, because it is plain text. Check what the scripts do, check what the instructions ask the agent to fetch, and check the allowed-tools field in particular: it is honoured even in an untrusted folder, so a skill can grant itself broad tool access before you have approved anything.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How long should a SKILL.md file be?</div><div class="a">Under 500 lines and roughly 5,000 tokens, per the specification's own recommendation. Longer files are not just expensive, they work worse: official guidance warns that overly comprehensive skills can hurt more than they help, because the agent struggles to extract what is relevant to the task at hand.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">When is a skill not worth writing?</div><div class="a">When the agent already does the job well. The official best-practice guidance says to test every instruction against whether the agent would get it wrong without it, and states directly that if the agent already handles the task well, the skill may not be adding value.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cav">Where this came from</div>
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li><a href="https://agentskills.io/specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Skills specification</a>: the directory structure, the six frontmatter fields and their constraints, and the three levels of progressive disclosure with token guidance.</li>
      <li><a href="https://agentskills.io/client-implementation/adding-skills-support.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Skills client implementation guide</a>: the per-tier token costs, and the note that the specification does not mandate where skill directories live.</li>
      <li><a href="https://agentskills.io/skill-creation/best-practices.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Skills best practices</a>: the 500-line ceiling, the warning about overly comprehensive skills, and the statement that a skill may add no value.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Agent Skills repository</a>: Apache 2.0 for code, CC BY 4.0 for documentation, and the origin statement.</li>
      <li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code skills documentation</a>: the install paths, the portable-versus-vendor field split, the persistence caveat, and the allowed-tools warning.</li>
      <li><a href="https://claude.com/blog/skills-explained" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Skills explained</a> (5 March 2026): the quoted comparisons against prompts, projects, MCP and subagents.</li>
      <li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Skills overview</a>: the progressive disclosure architecture and the trusted-sources security warning.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic engineering, Agent Skills</a> (16 October 2025, updated 18 December 2025): the open-standard publication.</li>
      <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10338" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agent Skills in the Wild</a> (arXiv, January 2026): 42,447 skills collected, 31,132 analysed, 26.1% with at least one vulnerability, and the 2.12x figure for skills bundling scripts.</li>
      <li><a href="https://snyk.io/blog/toxicskills-malicious-ai-agent-skills-clawhub/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToxicSkills</a> (February 2026): 3,984 skills scanned, 13.4% with a critical issue, 76 malicious payloads confirmed by human review.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.catonetworks.com/blog/cato-ctrl-weaponizing-claude-skills-with-medusalocker/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Weaponizing skills</a> (December 2025): the proof-of-concept modifying a published skill to run ransomware under the same approval.</li>
      <li><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/agent-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VS Code agent skills</a>, <a href="https://cursor.com/docs/context/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cursor</a>, <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/about-agent-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Copilot</a> and <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI</a>: independent vendor confirmation of SKILL.md support.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.linuxfoundation.org/press/linux-foundation-announces-the-formation-of-the-agentic-ai-foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Linux Foundation Agentic AI Foundation</a> (December 2025): the founding contributions, which include MCP and not Agent Skills.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/anthropics/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The reference skills repository</a>: star count as read in August 2026.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="toolsrow">Read next from Best Answer Hub: <a href="/playbooks/make-videos-with-claude-code/">making videos with Claude Code</a>, <a href="/playbooks/free-ai-voice-over-commercial-use/">free AI voice overs you can sell</a>, <a href="/playbooks/local-ai-video-no-gpu/">local AI video without a graphics card</a>, and <a href="/playbooks/ai-voice-youtube-monetization/">AI voice and YouTube monetization</a>. The packs live at <a href="/ai-agent-skills/">AI Agent Skills</a>.</p>
